\description{
Fits a single univariate Mixed Poisson (MP) or Zero-Inflated Mixed
Poisson (ZIMP) regression model for a specified mixing distribution and
variance-power parameterization.

Both formula and matrix interfaces are supported. The count component
models the mean of the Poisson mixing distribution, while an optional
zero-inflation component models the probability that the count-generating
component is active.
}
\details{
\code{fit_zimp()} fits one specified model. It does not perform model selection
over mixing distributions or variance-power specifications. For model
selection, see \code{\link[=select_zimp]{select_zimp()}}.

The conditional count model is

\deqn{Y \mid \Lambda \sim \mathrm{Poisson}(\Lambda),}

where the distribution of the latent intensity \eqn{\Lambda} is determined
by \code{mixing.distribution}. The marginal mean is linked to the count
covariates through

\deqn{\mu_i = \exp(x_i^\top \beta + o_i),}

where \eqn{o_i} is an optional count offset.

For a zero-inflated model, let \eqn{B_i} denote the indicator that the
count-generating component is active. The model uses

\deqn{Y_i = B_i N_i,}

with

\deqn{\Pr(B_i = 1) = \pi_i,\qquad
      \mathrm{logit}(\pi_i) = z_i^\top \gamma.}

Thus, \code{Z} models the probability of belonging to the count-generating
component rather than the structural-zero component.

The dispersion parameter \code{phi} is constrained to be positive and is
optimized internally on the log scale.
}
\section{Model specification}{


Two input interfaces are available.

\strong{Formula interface}

A Mixed Poisson model can be specified as

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{y ~ x1 + x2
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

and a Zero-Inflated Mixed Poisson model as

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{y ~ x1 + x2 | z1 + z2
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The expression before \code{|} specifies the count component and the expression
after \code{|} specifies the zero-inflation component.

In the \code{mzimp} formula interface, intercepts are included only when
explicitly requested with \code{1}. For example,

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{y ~ x1 + x2
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

specifies a count model without an intercept, whereas

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{y ~ 1 + x1 + x2
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

includes an intercept. The same convention applies independently to the
zero-inflation component.

\strong{Matrix interface}

The response and design matrices can instead be supplied directly through
\code{Y}, \code{X}, and optionally \code{Z}. If \code{Z = NULL}, an MP model is fitted. If \code{Z}
is supplied, a ZIMP model is fitted. Intercept columns, when required, must
be included explicitly in \code{X} and \code{Z}.
}

\section{Mixing distributions}{


The following mixing distributions are supported:

\describe{
\item{\code{"gamma"}}{Gamma mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"lnorm"}}{Lognormal mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"ig"}}{Inverse Gaussian mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"weibull"}}{Weibull mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"loglogistic"}}{Log-logistic mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"betaprime"}}{Beta-prime mixing distribution.}
}

The optional \code{var.power} argument controls the variance-power
parameterization. If \code{var.power = NULL}, the native/default
parameterization of the selected mixing distribution is used. Otherwise,
\code{var.power} must be one of \code{1}, \code{2}, or \code{3}.
}

\section{Initial values}{


Initial parameter values may be supplied through \code{ini.par}. Parameters are
ordered internally as

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{c(beta, log(phi), gamma)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

where \code{beta} contains the count-component coefficients, \code{log(phi)} is the
log-dispersion parameter, and \code{gamma} contains the zero-component
coefficients. The \code{gamma} block is omitted for an MP model.
}

\examples{
## Generate a small Mixed Poisson data set
set.seed(12345)
n <- 50
x1 <- rnorm(n)
x2 <- rnorm(n)
X <- cbind(1, x1, x2)
mu <- exp(X \%*\% c(0.5, 0.3, 0.7))

## Gamma-mixed Poisson data are Negative Binomial marginally.
## This provides a short reproducible example without relying on
## non-exported simulation functions.
phi <- 0.2
y <- rnbinom(n, size = 1 / phi, mu = mu)

dat <- data.frame(y = y, x1 = x1, x2 = x2)

## Formula interface
fit_formula <- fit_zimp(
  y ~ 1 + x1 + x2,
  data = dat,
  mixing.distribution = "gamma",
  var.power = 2,
  progress = FALSE
)

fit_formula

## Equivalent matrix interface
fit_matrix <- fit_zimp(
  Y = y,
  X = X,
  mixing.distribution = "gamma",
  var.power = 2,
  progress = FALSE
)

}
